  "summary": "A design style characterized by bold colors, space-age elements, and a sense of luxury and optimism in post-WWII consumer culture.",
  "description": "It features extravagant ornamentation, bright color palettes, and futuristic forms that appealed to American consumers' fascination with technology and luxury during the economic boom of the 1950s and 60s.",
  "culturalContext": "Thomas Hine's coined term (1986) for the exuberant American style of 1954-1964, when postwar prosperity met space-age optimism: Cadillac tailfins, boomerang Formica, starburst clocks and roadside signage. Distinct from Atompunk's genre framing, Populuxe is the documented historical design moment — washing machines as jet planes, kitchens as mission control — sold to suburban optimism with color names like Flamingo Pink and Turquoise.",
